TI  - Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) observation on the courtship behaviour of Indian Ocean humpback dolphins (Sousa plumbea) off Cochin, Arabian sea, India
T2  - REGIONAL STUDIES IN MARINE SCIENCE
LA  - English
KW  - Coastal dolphins
KW  - Marine megafauna
KW  - Mammal fishery interaction
KW  - MOTHER-CALF PAIRS
KW  - CHINENSIS
AB  - In India, the Indian Ocean humpback dolphin (Sousa plumbea) represents a significant coastal cetacean species exhibiting frequent interactions with fisheries. Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) observations were made to record the interaction and behavioural response of cetaceans with fishing systems off Cochin, in the Arabian Sea, India. Observations of courtship behaviour in Indian Ocean humpback dolphins (Sousa plumbea) were extracted from a 3.09-minute video recording. A pod of four individuals comprising two distinct pairs exhibited synchronized swimming, leaping, and surface splashing, consistent with courtship displays. The mating behaviour was lasted 24-29 s forming a single copulatory cycle. This research marks the first UAV-based observation of Indian Ocean humpback dolphin's courtship behaviour in the Asian region. Drone based observation helps guide conservation plans and management initiatives by offering a noninvasive understanding of how marine mammals behave in response to different environamental and phusical factors
