TY  - JOUR
AU  - COCKCROFT, VG
TI  - DOLPHIN CATCHES IN THE NATAL SHARK NETS, 1980 TO 1988
T2  - SOUTH AFRICAN JOURNAL OF WILDLIFE RESEARCH
LA  - English
AB  - Between January 1980 and December 1988, 279 Indian Ocean bottlenose dolphins Tursiops truncatus, 316 common dolphins Delphinus delphis and 67 indopacific humpback dolphins Sousa plumbea were caught in shark nets off Natal. Four other species of dolphin (Stenella longirostris, Stenella coeruleoalba, Lagenodelphis hosei and Pseudorca crassidens) were less frequently captured. Humpback and bottlenose dolphins were captured throughout the year reflecting their year-round residence in the Natal inshore zone. In contrast, common dolphin captures occurred predominantly in winter and were closely associated with the Natal ''sardine run''. The annual catch of common dolphins showed significant interannual variation, probably a function of the extent of the ''sardine run''. Captures of bottlenose and common dolphins occurred at most netted beaches off Natal, the level of catch being well correlated with the number of nets set at any particular beach. The majority of humpback dolphin catches occurred in northern Natal, probably indicating their relative abundance in this area. Similar numbers of males and females were captured of both humpback and bottlenose dolphins. Captures of the former were dominated by adolescent and mature dolphins, while captures of calves and mature females dominated the latter. Significantly more female than male common dolphins were captured. The implications and significance of the incidental death of dolphins of these species is discussed.
